// spec: an rvalue reference parameter given an lvalue of its own type is an error, as in C++ ([dcl.init.ref] 5.4.4; clang: "rvalue reference to type 'S' cannot bind to lvalue of type 'S'"): move(a) says the object may be moved from, a plain `a` never does; one of another type converts to a temporary, which it binds (classes/rvalue_reference_takes_a_converted_temporary), and so does a function lvalue (templates/forwarding_reference_to_function)
// found by thread B (2026-11-05): kpp bound the variable itself, so `take(a)` with `take(S&&)` left a moved from (`[]`) and `takeInt(i)` with `takeInt(int&&)` changed i (`5`), where the program is no C++
// error: rvalue_reference_given_lvalue_is_error.kpp:16: error: a S&& parameter given an lvalue of its type: an rvalue reference binds only a temporary or what move() gives, never the object itself
// error: rvalue_reference_given_lvalue_is_error.kpp:22: error: a int32&& parameter given an lvalue of its type: an rvalue reference binds only a temporary or what move() gives, never the object itself
class S
{
public:
  String s;
};
void take(S&& x) { S y = move(x); }
void takeInt(int&& v) { v = 5; }
String moved()
{
  S a;
  a.s = "hi";
  take(a);
  return a.s;
}
int changed()
{
  int i = 1;
  takeInt(i);
  return i;
}
int main()
{
  S b;
  take(move(b));
  takeInt(3);
  println("[", moved(), "] ", changed());
  return 0;
}
